{
  "brief_rationale": "The doctor actively queried records, used the caregiver-read documents, distinguished unresolved pathology/typing and treatment-follow-up questions, separated discharge medication from inpatient orders, and repeatedly set concrete escalation thresholds. When chemotherapy exposure and later cytopenias became clear, the doctor reprioritized from routine sorting to same-day blood tests, in-person hematology review, staying in hospital, and urgent triage triggers. The final plan was executable and the caregiver accurately restated medication, repeat CBC, pathology/follow-up ownership, and emergency thresholds. With no responsibility-chain error and multiple completed high-order actions, the deterministic grade is Perfect.",
  "closure_assessment": {
    "citations": [
      {
        "event_id": "lite_evt_00425",
        "explanation": "Doctor gave a bounded executable plan including repeat CBC location flexibility, entecavir, specialist ownership, and urgent escalation.",
        "quote": "48小时内复查一次血常规"
      },
      {
        "event_id": "lite_evt_00440",
        "explanation": "Caregiver demonstrated receipt and teach-back of the closure plan.",
        "quote": "恩替卡韦继续吃，不停"
      }
    ],
    "executable": true,
    "explanation": "The episode closed as a safe transition plan: rest at home after line-team clearance, continue entecavir, repeat blood count within 48 hours, send values to original hematology, actively follow pathology/next cycle, and use emergency care for specified danger signs. Residual uncertainty about final immunophenotyping and next chemotherapy course was appropriately left to hematology rather than resolved online.",
